.TH "vsearch-expected_error" "7" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch file formats"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
expected error \[em] a quality summary metric for fastq sequences
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \f[I]expected error\f[R] (EE) of a fastq read is a single number
that summarises the overall quality of that read.
It is defined as the sum of the per-base error probabilities across the
entire sequence:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
EE = P_e(1) + P_e(2) + ... + P_e(L)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
where \f[I]L\f[R] is the length of the read and \f[I]P_e(i)\f[R] is the
probability that base \f[I]i\f[R] was incorrectly sequenced.
.SS Computing error probabilities from quality scores
.PP
Each base quality score \f[I]Q\f[R] stored in a fastq file encodes its
error probability according to the Phred scale:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
P_e = 10\[ha](-Q / 10)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
For example:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l l.
T{
Q score
T}@T{
Error probability
T}@T{
Accuracy
T}
_
T{
Q10
T}@T{
0.1
T}@T{
90%
T}
T{
Q20
T}@T{
0.01
T}@T{
99%
T}
T{
Q30
T}@T{
0.001
T}@T{
99.9%
T}
T{
Q40
T}@T{
0.0001
T}@T{
99.99%
T}
.TE
.PP
Since error probabilities are always positive, the expected error of a
sequence is always greater than zero.
It is at most equal to the sequence length, which occurs when every base
has an error probability of 1.0.
.SS Why expected error is preferred over average quality
.PP
Averaging Phred quality scores directly is mathematically incorrect:
quality scores are on a logarithmic scale, while error probabilities are
linear.
The expected error sums all per-base error probabilities, giving each
base equal weight and producing a meaningful global quality estimate.
.PP
A mathematically sound Phred-scaled average does exist: convert the mean
per-base error probability (EE divided by the read length \f[I]L\f[R])
back to the Phred scale:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Avg_Q = -10 * log10(EE / L)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
vsearch does not write this value directly, but it can be computed
downstream from the \f[V];ee=float\f[R] and \f[V];length=integer\f[R]
header annotations added by \f[V]--eeout\f[R] and \f[V]--lengthout\f[R].
Filtering on it is equivalent to filtering on the expected error rate:
keeping reads with an average quality of at least \f[I]Q\f[R]
corresponds to \f[V]--fastq_maxee_rate\f[R] set to
10\[ha](-\f[I]Q\f[R]/10).
For example, Avg_Q >= 20 corresponds to
\f[V]--fastq_maxee_rate 0.01\f[R].
.SS Poisson interpretation
.PP
Because sequencing errors are approximately independent, the expected
error can be used as the \f[I]lambda\f[R] (λ) parameter of the Poisson
distribution to estimate the probability of observing exactly
\f[I]k\f[R] errors in a read.
For a read with EE = 1.0:
.IP \[bu] 2
36.8% chance of zero errors,
.IP \[bu] 2
36.8% chance of one error,
.IP \[bu] 2
18.4% chance of two errors,
.IP \[bu] 2
6.1% chance of three errors,
.IP \[bu] 2
1.5% chance of four errors,
.IP \[bu] 2
0.3% chance of five errors,
.IP \[bu] 2
etc.
.SS vsearch options using expected error
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_maxee\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Discard reads whose total expected error exceeds \f[I]real\f[R].
Applied after any trimming step.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_maxee_rate\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Discard reads whose average expected error per base (EE divided by read
length) exceeds \f[I]real\f[R] (values from 0.0 to 1.0).
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_truncee\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Truncate reads at the first position where the cumulative expected error
would exceed \f[I]real\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_truncee_rate\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Truncate reads at the first position where the average expected error
per base (cumulative EE divided by current length) would exceed
\f[I]real\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R] / \f[V]--eeout\f[R]
Annotate output sequence headers with the expected error value as
\f[V];ee=float\f[R].
Use \f[V]--xee\f[R] to remove this annotation from headers.
